.TH CHTTPSVR_START 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_start \- register a listener with the running engine
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_start(chttpsvr srv,
                             const chttpsvr_config_t *cfg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Starts the server on the port and the host that cfg names.  cfg may be
NULL, and the server then uses the defaults of
CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT.

cfg->host names the TCP listener, with the rules of the listener of
the Go net package:

  NULL or ""     (NULL is the default of CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT)
                 every interface, with one dual-stack socket on [::]
                 (IPV6_V6ONLY off) that also takes IPv4 clients through
                 v4-mapped addresses; 0.0.0.0 on a host without IPv6.
                 cfg->ipv6_only makes it an IPv6-only listener.
  an address     exactly that address: "0.0.0.0" is every IPv4
                 interface, "::" every IPv6 interface, "127.0.0.1" the
                 IPv4 loopback alone.  An address in brackets, as in
                 "[::1]", is the address inside the brackets; a name
                 in brackets fails the start.
  a host name    its first IPv4 address when it has one, else its first
                 IPv6 address; "localhost" listens on 127.0.0.1.  With
                 cfg->ipv6_only, its first IPv6 address when it has
                 one, else its first IPv4 address.

The server tries only that one address.  When it cannot bind it, for
example because another socket holds the port on that address, the
start fails with ccol_unexpected_failure; the server never listens on
another address of the same name in its place.  A host name that does
not resolve, or a malformed bracketed address, fails the start in the
same way, and the engine logger reports the reason.

cfg->host can also be a "unix://path/to/socket" string, in which case
the server binds a Unix domain socket at that path and ignores
cfg->port.  A socket file at that path on which no process listens is
replaced.  The start fails with ccol_unexpected_failure, leaving the
file unchanged, when:
  - The path names an object that is not a socket.
  - The path names a socket on which a live server listens.
chttpsvr_stop and the destroy remove the socket file only while the
path names the socket that this server created, and they reach the
file through the directory that held it at start time, so a later
chdir() has no effect.  One chttpsvr instance listens either on TCP or
on a Unix socket, never on both; to use both, create two instances.

An empty path fails the start with ccol_unexpected_failure, and so
does a path that does not fit in the sun_path field of struct
sockaddr_un, which holds 108 bytes on Linux and 104 bytes on the BSDs,
including the terminating NUL.  A relative path is resolved against
the working directory at the time of the start.

If the shared ccol_event_loop reactor of the process, and the
idle-timeout sweep thread beside it, are not running yet, this call
starts them and blocks until the library confirms that they are
running before it continues.  This reactor is separate from the engine
of chttpclient, and starting or stopping one has no effect on the
other.  The function then binds the listener socket synchronously and
returns once the socket is in the LISTEN state and registered with the
reactor.

You can start more than one chttpsvr handle at the same time, on
different ports or on different Unix socket paths, and all of them
share the same reactor.

A port of 0 is rejected with ccol_invalid_args, except when cfg->host
is a "unix://" path, where the port has no meaning and is ignored.

At start time the server creates a ctpool, through which it dispatches
every buffered handler.  The pool has cfg->worker_thread_count
threads, and its queue capacity is cfg->worker_queue_capacity, where 0
means 1024 slots per thread and CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED means no
capacity limit.  Streaming handlers run on a second pool of their
own, which the server creates when it receives its first streaming
request (see "Slow clients" below); setting
cfg->streaming_thread_count to CHTTPSVR_STREAMING_POOL_OFF runs them
on the worker pool instead.  The server owns both pools and destroys
them when the server is destroyed.

Rejection of a route: the server rejects a route that does not match,
or that is malformed, with 404, 405 or 500, and decides this before it
reads any body byte.  A matched route whose ctpool is at capacity is
rejected with 503 and a "Retry-After: 5" header.  Both get a courtesy
error response, and any middleware of that rejection runs on a small
dedicated pool of the server's own.  No thread ever waits for the
client to read that response: a write that meets a full socket parks
the connection, without a thread, until the client reads, and the
internal ceiling of 2 seconds (see max_response_write_duration_us)
ends it.  A slow-reading client that gets a "no" therefore cannot hold
a thread or delay the dispatch of any other, unrelated connection.
The interim "100 Continue" line of a buffered route parks in the same
way, and the body is read once the line is out.

Lingering close: when the server closes a connection after a complete
response while the client may still be sending (the unread body of a
refused request, a body that a handler left unread, or pipelined
bytes), a plain close would make the kernel answer with a reset, which
can destroy the response before the client reads it.  Instead, the
server sends a TLS close_notify when the connection uses TLS, shuts
down its side of the connection for writing, and reads and discards
whatever the client still sends, for at most 2 seconds, before it
closes.  The client therefore reads the whole response followed by an
orderly end of stream, and so does a client that sends the whole body
of a refused upload before it reads the response, as long as it
finishes sending within that time.  A lingering connection holds no
thread, and it counts against cfg->max_connections until it closes.  A
refused request whose framing has no body, with nothing more in the
socket, is closed at once.

Limit on the body size: cfg->max_body_size caps the request body, and
0 means unlimited.  A buffered route whose body exceeds it is rejected
with 413 before the handler runs.  The handler of a streaming route is
always called; for a streaming route, chttpsvr_req_read() returns -1
and chttpsvr_req_stream_error() reports ccol_msg_too_large once the
body crosses the limit.  In both cases the server closes the
connection after the response (Connection: close) instead of keeping
it alive, because it does not read the body bytes beyond the limit.
That close is a lingering close; see "Lingering close" above.

A buffered route whose declared Content-Length already exceeds
max_body_size is rejected with 413 at once, without waiting for a body
byte first, since a stalled or malicious peer may never send one.
This does not apply to a streaming route: the handler of a streaming
route is always called, whatever the declared Content-Length, which
matches the contract above.  One more check applies regardless of the
route kind: the server also rejects any single chunk of a chunked
Transfer-Encoding whose declared size exceeds max_body_size, as soon
as it parses the chunk-size line of that chunk and before it waits
for the data of that chunk.  This shows up in the same way as any
other malformed body for that route kind, that is, as a 413 or as
ccol_msg_too_large.

Every timeout and duration of chttpsvr_config_t is a uint64_t count of
microseconds, rounded up to whole milliseconds inside the server; see
CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT(3).

Timeouts for a body read: cfg->stream_read_timeout_us bounds the gap
between two batches of body bytes, for a buffered body and for a
streaming body alike, and a value of 0 waits forever.  Because it
resets on every new byte, a client that sends its data just fast
enough never trips it, so cfg->max_body_read_duration_us separately
bounds the *total* time that the server spends reading the body of one
request, whatever the progress in each gap.  Its default is 300000000,
that is, 5 minutes, and 0 turns it off; see chttpsvr_req_read.
cfg->response_write_timeout_us bounds the equivalent gap on the
response-write side, where 0 means the value of
stream_read_timeout_us.  It bounds how long a response can wait on a
client that stops reading, and the send queue of the socket shrinking
counts as progress; see CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT(3).
cfg->max_response_write_duration_us is the write-side equivalent of
max_body_read_duration_us: its default is 300000000, that is, 5
minutes, 0 turns it off, and it separately bounds the *total* time
that the server spends sending one response, whatever the progress in
each call.  When it expires, the send of the response fails and the
server closes the connection.

For a buffered route, no thread waits out any of these four limits: a
body that stops arriving, or a response that meets a full socket,
parks its connection without a thread (see "Slow clients" below), and
the limits then apply to the parked connection.  The idle-timeout
sweep thread checks them once a second, and the server checks them
again when the connection becomes ready, so a client that stalls past
a limit and then comes back gets the same answer.  An expired body
gets 408 Request Timeout and a close, and an expired response is cut
off and closed.  A streaming handler waits on its own thread inside
chttpsvr_req_read(), where the limits bound that wait directly.

Slow clients: for a buffered route, a worker thread never waits on
the socket of a client.  When the body of a request stops arriving,
the worker parks the connection, which then holds no thread, and
returns to the pool.  When more of the body is ready, the reactor
hands the connection to any free worker, which continues where the
other one stopped.  A response write that meets a full socket parks
the connection in the same way, and a later worker writes the rest.
Keep-alive, pipelining, "Expect: 100-continue", HEAD, 204 and 304 work
across a park exactly as without one.  A parked body asks the kernel
to wake the reactor only once the rest of the body, or 16 KiB of it,
is queued (SO_RCVLOWAT), so a client that sends one byte at a time
costs one wake-up rather than one per byte.

cfg->min_transfer_rate_bps sets a minimum average rate, 240 bytes a
second by default, for the body of a request and for the response to
it.  The rate is measured from the start of the body read, or of the
response write, and judged once cfg->min_transfer_rate_grace_us,
5000000 (5 s) by default, has passed.  A body below the floor gets 408
Request Timeout and a close; a response below it is cut off and
closed; a streaming handler sees chttpsvr_req_read() fail, with
chttpsvr_req_stream_error() reporting ccol_timed_out.  Time during
which the server itself holds a request back, waiting for body memory
or in the streaming queue, never counts against the client.
CHTTPSVR_NO_RATE_FLOOR turns the floor off.

cfg->max_partial_body_memory, 256 MiB by default, caps the memory that
the bodies of buffered requests on this server hold together, from
the first byte that the server reads until the handler returns.  A
body with a Content-Length reserves its whole length before the server
reads a byte of it; when it does not fit, the request waits without a
thread, the server reads nothing from it, and it sends no "100
Continue" until memory frees.  A chunked body reserves its buffer as
the buffer grows, including the old buffer while a growth copies it,
and waits in the same way when it runs out.  The buffer of a body with
a Content-Length takes exactly that length, so a reservation always
covers the memory that the body holds.  Waiting requests are admitted
strictly in the order in which they began to wait, so a large body is
never starved by small ones.  When every body that holds memory is
itself waiting for more, the oldest waiter goes ahead past the limit,
one request at a time, so the limit is exceeded by less than twice
max_body_size and the server never deadlocks.  The server frees a
body, and returns its reservation, as soon as its handler returns and
before the response goes out, so a response that waits for a slow
reader holds no body; a request that is refused, cut off or closed
frees both at once.  A body whose handler is running is not waiting,
so a request that a handler waits for, and whose body does not fit
beside the bodies that running handlers hold, waits until its
deadline.  A request that waits longer than
cfg->body_memory_wait_timeout_us, 30000000 (30 s) by default, gets 503
Service Unavailable with "Retry-After: 5" and a close.  A body that
arrives complete together with its headers, and every streaming
route, reserves nothing.  CHTTPSVR_NO_MEMORY_CAP turns the limit off,
and CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE removes the deadline of the wait.

Streaming handlers run on their own pool of
cfg->streaming_thread_count threads (the worker thread count by
default), so slow streaming clients can occupy at most that pool and
never the threads that serve buffered routes.  A streaming request
that finds no free streaming thread waits, without a thread, in a
queue of cfg->streaming_queue_capacity entries (four times the
streaming thread count by default).  A request that finds the queue
full gets 503 with "Retry-After: 5" at once, and one that waits longer
than cfg->streaming_queue_timeout_us, 5000000 (5 s) by default, gets
the same answer.  A handler never runs for a request that got that
503.

Every limit here reads 0 as its default, so a configuration that
never mentions a field keeps the defense; each one is turned off only
through its own named constant, from <ccollections/chttpserver.h>:

  CHTTPSVR_NO_RATE_FLOOR       ((unsigned)-1), for min_transfer_rate_bps:
                               no minimum rate; a body read or a
                               response write is then bounded only by
                               stream_read_timeout_us,
                               max_body_read_duration_us,
                               response_write_timeout_us and
                               max_response_write_duration_us.
  CHTTPSVR_NO_MEMORY_CAP       ((size_t)-1), for max_partial_body_memory:
                               no limit on body memory, so no request
                               waits for it.
  CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE         UINT64_MAX, for body_memory_wait_timeout_us
                               and streaming_queue_timeout_us: the wait
                               lasts as long as it takes.  It holds no
                               thread, and a destroy of the server still
                               ends it.  Every other value, however
                               large, is a finite deadline.
  CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED     ((size_t)-1), for worker_queue_capacity and
                               streaming_queue_capacity: no limit on the
                               queue, so no request gets a 503 because the
                               queue is full.
  CHTTPSVR_STREAMING_POOL_OFF  (-1), for streaming_thread_count:
                               streaming handlers run on the worker
                               pool, with no pool and no queue of their
                               own.  worker_queue_capacity then bounds
                               them, and a slow streaming client can
                               hold a worker thread for as long as the
                               read limits allow.

The 503 that the server writes itself always carries "Retry-After: 5".

Idle timeout of a connection: cfg->read_timeout_us and
cfg->idle_timeout_us are applied by the idle-timeout sweep thread of
this module, which every chttpsvr instance in the process shares; no
OS timer per connection applies these two fields.  idle_timeout_us
takes precedence over read_timeout_us when idle_timeout_us is above 0;
otherwise the server uses read_timeout_us, and a value of 0 in both
turns the idle timeout off completely.  This bounds only the time that
a connection spends idle between requests, waiting for the next
pipelined or keep-alive request, and never the time that a worker
thread spends handling a request.

Limit on the duration of a header read:
cfg->max_header_read_duration_us bounds the *total* wall-clock time
that one connection can spend in the reactor-owned phase of one
request, which is the TLS handshake, if there is one, plus the read of
the whole header block of that request.  Its default is 30000000, that
is, 30 s, and 0 turns it off.  read_timeout_us and idle_timeout_us
above reset on every arriving byte, so a peer that sends its request
headers, or its handshake, one byte at a time, without ever completing
them, defeats both, and such a peer never comes near max_header_bytes
either.  This field is the header-phase equivalent of
max_body_read_duration_us and is applied by the same idle-timeout
sweep thread, which runs once a second and closes every expired
connection, however many there are, so a connection can live up to
about a second longer than its deadline.  See
CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT(3).  The budget is per request: it starts at
the first handshake step, or at the first byte of the header block of
the request when there is no handshake, and the next request of a
keep-alive connection starts a fresh budget when its own first byte
arrives.  When the budget expires, the server closes the connection
without sending a response, because the header block was never
complete enough to answer.

Limit on the header size: cfg->max_header_bytes bounds the combined
size of the header block of one request, and 0 means the library
default of 64 KiB.  A request whose headers exceed it is answered with
400 Bad Request and the connection is then closed, in the same way as
the request parser of this module reports every other malformed
request before routing.  The answer is best effort: a peer that is
still streaming its oversized header block when the server closes may
hit a reset before it reads anything.  Whatever this value, the
request line and each header line must end within 8 KiB, CRLF
included, and a request holds at most 100 header lines.

Request validation: the server refuses the following requests before
any route, middleware or handler sees them, and closes the connection
after the answer.  A request line whose HTTP version is not 1.x, such
as HTTP/0.9, HTTP/2.0 or HTTP/3.0, gets 505 HTTP Version Not
Supported, while a higher minor version of HTTP/1 is served as
HTTP/1.1 (RFC 9110 section 2.5).  A Host header outside the uri-host
[ ":" port ] grammar of RFC 9112 section 3.2 gets 400: the host must
be a reg-name of unreserved, sub-delims and percent-encoded bytes, an
IPv4 address, or a bracketed IP literal, so a '/', '@', '?', '#' or
whitespace, an empty host, or a port that is not all digits is
refused.  A Transfer-Encoding that names a coding other than chunked,
such as "gzip, chunked", gets 501 Not Implemented, because the server
decodes no other coding, while one that does not end in chunked gets
400.  The chunk framing of a body (size lines, chunk extensions and
CRLFs) may exceed 16 bytes plus twice the data of each chunk by 16
KiB in total; a body beyond that is malformed, so a buffered
route answers 400 and chttpsvr_req_read of a streaming route fails
with ccol_http_transfer_aborted.  An HTTP/1.0 request that carries
"Expect: 100-continue" gets no interim 100 response (RFC 9110 section
10.1.1).

Request target: the server accepts an origin-form target, which is a
path that begins with "/", and an absolute-form target with the scheme
http or https, whose path it serves and whose authority it ignores; an
absolute-form target with an empty path stands for "/".  Every other
target, including a path with no leading "/", a bare query such as
"?x", and any other scheme, gets 400 Bad Request and a close, before
the route search and before any middleware runs.

The asterisk form follows Go's net/http server exactly.  The server
answers "OPTIONS *" itself with 200 OK and a Content-Length of 0, and
runs no route, no middleware and no handler.  The response carries the
same headers as any other response (date, content-length and
connection) and no Allow header, and the connection follows the
ordinary keep-alive rules.
The server reads and discards a request body of up to 4 KiB; a larger
body, or one that fails to arrive, still gets the 200, and the
connection is closed after it.  A declared Content-Length above 4 KiB
is not read at all, and no 100 Continue invites it.  Any other method
with the target "*", including a method outside the seven
chttp_method_t names such as "PROPFIND *", gets 400 Bad Request and a
close.  A method outside those seven names with any other target gets
501 Not Implemented.

Date: unless a handler set its own Date with
chttpsvr_resp_set_header(3), each final response has a Date header
with the current time in UTC, in the IMF-fixdate form of RFC 9110
section 5.6.7.  This applies to handler responses, HEAD, each
rejection that the server writes itself, "OPTIONS *", and each
response on a keep-alive connection.  A 1xx response and the interim
100 Continue have no Date, as in the net/http server of Go.  The
headers that the server adds itself are therefore:
  - date.
  - content-length, except on a 1xx, a 204, a 304, or a HEAD
    response for which the handler wrote no body.
  - connection.
  - allow on a 405, and retry-after on a 503 that the server writes
    itself.

Host: a request with more than one Host header gets 400 Bad Request,
for all versions, and so does an HTTP/1.1 request with no Host header;
an HTTP/1.0 request does not need one.  With two Host lines, a
front-end could forward one authority while this server reads a
different one.

The request target must not contain whitespace (a space or a
horizontal tab), other control characters or DEL; a request line that
breaks this rule gets 400 Bad Request and a close, before routing.

Framing: the server reads Transfer-Encoding as one list, merged from
all Transfer-Encoding lines, and applies these rules:
  - chunked must be the last coding of that list.
  - chunked must occur in the list at most once.
  - A Transfer-Encoding together with a Content-Length is refused.
A request that breaks one of these rules gets 400 Bad Request and a
close, before routing.

A malformed body is a client error, so it gets 400, not 500.  These
bodies are malformed:
  - A body with a chunk size that is not hexadecimal.
  - A body with a chunk that has no CRLF at its end.
  - A body with a malformed trailer line.
  - A body that ends before the length that its framing gives.
A body above max_body_size gets 413, not 400, and a body that stops
arriving gets 408, not 400.

An HTTP/1.0 request that has Transfer-Encoding gets 400 Bad Request and
a close, whatever its Connection header says, as RFC 9112 section 6.1
requires.  A chunk-size line must follow the full chunk-extension
grammar of RFC 9112 section 7.1.1, and a control byte in an extension
makes the body malformed, so the request gets 400.  A "close" token in
a Connection header closes the connection after the response, for all
HTTP versions, even when the header also has a "keep-alive" token.

Limit on the connection count: cfg->max_connections bounds how many
connections this listener can have open at one time, and 0 means
unlimited.  At capacity, new connections stay pending in the listen
backlog of the kernel instead of being accepted and then rejected.
The listener itself stops accepting until a slot frees, and then
resumes automatically, with a worst case of about one second between a
slot being freed and the listener noticing it.

A temporary shortage of resources pauses this listener in the same
way: when the process or the system is temporarily out of file
descriptors, or when a custom allocator from ccol_create_chttpsvr_mp
temporarily cannot serve an allocation for a connection, new
connections stay pending until a periodic sweep resumes the listener,
with the same worst case of one second.  This keeps a temporary
shortage on one listener from delaying any other, unrelated connection
on any other chttpsvr instance that shares the same reactor thread.

Socket options: the server sets TCP_NODELAY on every TCP connection
that it accepts and leaves the socket buffer sizes of such a
connection to the kernel, so that the buffer autotuning of Linux
(tcp(7)) stays on.  On a connection of a Unix domain socket listener,
which has no autotuning, it raises both socket buffers to at least 128
KiB.  The three configurable options below all default to false, are
applied on a best-effort basis, and do nothing on a Unix domain
socket listener.  cfg->enable_keepalive sets SO_KEEPALIVE on every
connection that the server accepts.  cfg->enable_reuseport sets
SO_REUSEPORT on the listening socket, which lets more than one
chttpsvr instance bind the same host and port, so that the kernel
load-balances the accepts.  cfg->ipv6_only sets IPV6_V6ONLY on an
AF_INET6 listener, so that the listener does not also accept
IPv4-mapped connections.

Restart: you can call chttpsvr_start again on the same handle after a
call to chttpsvr_stop; the new call registers a new listener with the
new configuration.  The call returns at once.  Fresh pools replace the
pools of the previous run immediately, and the old pools drain in the
background: a request of the previous run that is still running finishes
normally, with its own response, and the restart neither waits for it
nor cuts it off.  A keep-alive connection that stayed open across the
stop is served under the new configuration from its next request
on.  The server automatically frees any TLS context of the previous
cycle before it installs the new one; a connection that is still using
it keeps it until that connection closes.

Do not call chttpsvr_start from a request handler or a middleware of
srv to restart the very server that the handler runs on (such a
handler usually calls chttpsvr_stop first).  The library refuses such
a call with ccol_not_permitted rather than attempting it, and the
refusal leaves srv in a safe, recoverable state: the configuration
that srv had before, or a stopped server when chttpsvr_stop has
already run.  A later, legitimate call to chttpsvr_start from a
different thread still restarts it normally.

TLS: when cfg->tls is not NULL, it must have both cert_path and
key_path set.  Any other shape is rejected with ccol_invalid_args
before any other work is attempted, instead of silently starting the
server as plain, unencrypted HTTP.  Those other shapes are: exactly
one of the two set; cfg->tls->ca_bundle_path set with neither of the
two; and a cfg->tls that is otherwise empty, for example
CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT.  A trust store alone is never a valid server-side
configuration, and neither is an otherwise empty chttp_tls_config_t,
because the server side always needs an identity certificate for the
server.  The client side differs here: ca_bundle_path alone is the
ordinary way to set up verification against a custom CA with no client
certificate at all.

chttpsvr_start fails with ccol_unexpected_failure, and registers no
listener, when cert_path and key_path are both set but the library
cannot load the certificate and key pair.  The load fails for a file
that is missing or unreadable, for a path that does not name a regular
file (a directory, a FIFO or a device, which the library refuses at
once without waiting on it), for a file above the 16 MB ceiling under
which the library reads every PEM artefact, and for malformed
contents.  When set, cfg->tls->ca_bundle_path must also load
successfully, under the same rules, since that bundle is what turns on
mutual TLS on the resulting listener, and it must contribute at least
one certificate: a bundle that the library can read but that parses
to no certificate, or only to certificate revocation lists, is a load
failure, not an empty trust store.  A failure to load it is also
reported as ccol_unexpected_failure, rather than silently starting the
server without the client-certificate verification that you asked
for.

A ca_bundle_path that holds certificate revocation lists (CRLs) beside
its CA certificates enforces them: every certificate of the chain that
a client presents, below its trust anchor, then needs a CRL of its
issuer in the bundle, as the ssl_crl directive of nginx requires.  The
handshake fails for a revoked client certificate, for a certificate
whose issuer has no CRL in the bundle, and for a CRL past its next
update time.  The bundle is read here, so a renewed CRL takes effect at
the next chttpsvr_start.  See CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT(3).

By default, mutual TLS requires a client certificate: the handshake of
a client that presents none fails, and so does the handshake of a
client whose certificate does not verify against the bundle.
cfg->tls->client_cert_optional relaxes only the first rule, so the
server then also serves a client that presents no certificate.
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3) tells a handler which kind of
client it is serving, and chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_der(3),
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_sha256(3) and chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_subject(3)
tell it which client authenticated.

The listener resumes TLS sessions, from its session cache and from TLS
1.3 tickets, for TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3, with or without a ca_bundle_path,
and both for a client that presents a certificate and for one that
does not.  Each call of chttpsvr_start builds a new TLS configuration
with a new session cache, so a session from an earlier cycle ends in a
full handshake, never in a failure.

Pipelining: a client can write its next request before it reads the
previous response, and a keep-alive connection serves such a client on
buffered and streaming routes alike.  Bytes of a later request can
arrive in the same read as the headers or the body of the current
request; the server keeps those bytes for the next request and never
drops them.  Pipelined requests can arrive together in one TLS record,
and the server answers all of them without needing more bytes from the
client, exactly as over plain TCP.

Each response that has a body gives its length in Content-Length.  The
server sends a TLS close_notify before it closes a TLS connection
whose handshake is complete, so a client that treats a TLS end of
stream without close_notify as a truncation never sees a truncation
from this server.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID or a stale handle that
has already been destroyed; when cfg->port is 0 and cfg->host is not
a "unix://" path; when cfg->tls is set but cert_path and key_path are
not both set; or when cfg->streaming_thread_count is negative and not
CHTTPSVR_STREAMING_POOL_OFF.
ccol_not_permitted when this server has already started, or when this
call came from a request handler or a middleware of srv.
ccol_not_enough_memory when a memory allocation fails, or when the
library could not create the shared engine, that is, its reactor
threads or its own diagnostics logger.
ccol_unexpected_failure when the library could not create or bind the
listen socket (for example because another socket holds the port on
the address that cfg->host names, or because cfg->host does not
resolve or is malformed), could not register it with the shared
reactor, or could not start the shared idle-timeout sweep thread.
It is also returned when cfg->tls was set and the library could not load
its certificate and key pair or its ca_bundle_path, and when the process
already holds PTHREAD_KEYS_MAX thread-specific keys, so that the library
cannot create the key that marks the worker threads of a server; the
server then does not start, and a later call tries again.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library leaves the disposition of SIGPIPE to the application and
never changes it, here or anywhere else.  No write of the server
raises that signal: every write to a connection, including a TLS
record and the close_notify of a TLS teardown, is a send(2) or a
sendmsg(2) with MSG_NOSIGNAL.  A client that closes or resets its
connection while the server writes a response therefore ends only that
one connection, never the process, whatever disposition of SIGPIPE the
application has chosen.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
chttpsvr_stop closes only the listener socket.  A connection that the
server accepted before the stop keeps running and can still dispatch
more requests on this same handle at any point during a later
restart.  Every such request goes to the pools that the server is
running with at that moment, and a request running on a pool of the
previous run finishes there before that pool is destroyed.  A restart
is therefore safe to call while such a connection is still active, and
no request ever goes to a pool that is being torn down at the same
time.  A parked connection holds no thread; it stays with the server
and resumes on the new worker pool.  chttpsvr_destroy waits for the
requests of every run, with the bound that CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT(3)
describes.

A chttpsvr_start call that restarts srv is also safe when it races a
concurrent chttpsvr_stop call on the same handle from a different
thread: this function waits for the teardown of the old listener in
that stop to finish completely and then binds a new listener on the
same host and port, instead of racing a bind() against a socket that
the stop has not yet closed.

chttpsvr_start is also safe when it races a concurrent engine-wide
teardown, whether that comes from chttpsvr_engine_stop() or from the
graceful shutdown of the shared reactor after the destruction of the
last other server that references it: this function transparently
retries until that teardown finishes, rather than racing it.
.fi
